#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049248 :topic/difficulty 1.1438864763480574 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428353 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 2 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.666667; nearest selected fraction = 2/3. Crossing activity numbers [2, 3]; waits [2, 1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=2/3 (2/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1784->2978. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049178} :encompassing/weight 0.6666666666666666}] :topic/id #uuid "e6892f6e-116e-513d-8b41-f83291c0d8f3" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119510 :question/id #uuid "9bd80a60-0f80-5cc2-8193-86fb32c79913" :question/math-academy-id "e-12467" :question/problem "If $x=2y^2 + \\dfrac1{y^2}$, then find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4y-2{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =4y-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{4}-2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{y^3}{4y^4 -2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ac6edc-908a-50be-9ef1-4b24ba597d1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119940 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119511 :question/id #uuid "51e74bbf-6ce4-58c4-84b1-44a2f4c46ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-12468" :question/problem "If $x = y + 2y\\sqrt y$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ at the point $\\left(3 , 1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1+3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =1+3\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} =  \\dfrac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,1)} & =\\frac{1}{1+3\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119941 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119512 :question/id #uuid "330c7f67-4c32-5ab7-9909-1835bba1b1f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12469" :question/problem "What is the equation of the tangent to the curve $x = 2y^2 -y + 1$ at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2y)-1+0 \\\\  & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac1{4y - 1}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ff302ba-5a3f-599b-b038-7417af1dca71" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Tangent Line Using dx/dy"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1784 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048776}] :topic/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy"}]}
